.\" bluetooth(8) — NextBSD Bluetooth command-line client.
.\"
.\" NextBSD-original. There is no upstream page to vendor: Apple's equivalent
.\" surface lives inside the closed bluetoothd. This page is written for this
.\" tool.
.\"
.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt BLUETOOTH 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bluetooth
.Nd control Bluetooth
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm power
.Cm on | off
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm scan
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm pair
.Ar device
.Op Ar pin
.Op Fl -no-prompt
.Nm
.Cm confirm
.Ar device
.Cm yes | no | Ar passkey | Ar pin
.Nm
.Cm connect
.Ar device
.Nm
.Cm disconnect
.Ar device
.Nm
.Cm unpair
.Ar device
.Nm
.Cm device
.Ar device
.Nm
.Cm autoconnect
.Ar device
.Cm on | off
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Ar device
.Cm on | off
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Ar device
.Cm volume
.Ar level
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Ar device
.Cm hfp
.Op Cm cvsd
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Ar device
.Cm a2dp
.Nm
.Cm audio
.Cm autoswitch | sink
.Cm on | off
.Nm
.Cm discoverable
.Cm on | off
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm info
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm hci
.Op Fl n Ar node
.Ar ogf ocf
.Op Ar byte ...
.Nm
.Cm ping
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl i Ar seconds
.Ar device
.Nm
.Cm sdp | browse
.Ar device
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the command-line client for
.Xr bluetoothd 8 ,
the NextBSD Bluetooth daemon, and the only Bluetooth command on the system.
It covers everyday use (power, scan, pair, connect) and the diagnostics
that used to need separate tools: controller information, raw HCI commands,
L2CAP echo and SDP browsing.
.Pp
It is a pure-C Mach client: it looks up the
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth
Mach service and issues MIG RPCs.
It does not link CoreFoundation or SystemConfiguration, so it is also the
supported way for anything above the Mach line, including the Gershwin
desktop, to drive Bluetooth.
.Pp
A
.Ar device
is an address,
.Ql AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
(an LE random address carries a
.Ql /random
suffix, which may be left off when typing it), or the start of a device's name, ignoring case.
A name is looked up among the paired devices first, then among the devices
the last
.Cm scan
found; it must match one device, or exactly one by its whole name.
A
.Ar node
names a controller, such as
.Ql ubt0 ;
without one, the first controller is meant.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "status"
.It Cm status
Print the daemon's state, the persisted policy (power, discoverable,
auto-connect), the number of paired devices, the firmware loader's work,
each connected device with the profiles running over its link (HID, HID
over GATT, audio), and each controller with its transport, USB vendor and
product, state, address and name.
.It Cm power Cm on | off Op Ar node
Power a controller on or off.
Off disconnects every device and stops the controller answering pages and
inquiries.
The choice is saved as
.Ql ControllerPowerState
and applies to controllers that arrive later.
Bluetooth is on by default.
.It Cm scan Oo Fl t Ar seconds Oc Op Ar node
Discover nearby devices for
.Ar seconds
(default 10, at most 60), Classic and LE together, then print each one's
address, name, kind (from its class of device, or for LE its appearance),
link
.Pq Ql BR/EDR , Ql LE No or Ql dual ,
signal, and whether it is paired.
Prints
.Dq no devices found
when there were none.
Put a device in pairing mode before scanning for it.
.It Cm list
Print the paired devices: address, name, class of device, link, whether it
reconnects automatically, its connection state, and its profiles.
.It Cm pair Ar device Oo Ar pin Oc Op Fl -no-prompt
Pair with a device and follow the pairing to the end.
Classic or LE is chosen from what the scan saw.
A device with nothing to compare, such as a mouse or headphones, pairs with
no question: running
.Cm pair
was the yes.
If the device needs a number confirmed, a passkey or a PIN,
.Nm
asks on the terminal.
For a keyboard it prints a passkey: type it on the keyboard, then press
Enter there.
A legacy device that needs a PIN can be given it on the command line.
Ctrl-C refuses the pairing.
.Pp
Without a terminal, or with
.Fl -no-prompt ,
.Nm
does not ask: it prints one line,
.Dl address=<addr> method=<method> passkey=<digits> expires=<seconds>
and exits with status 3, and
.Cm confirm
answers.
.Ar method
is one of
.Ql numeric-comparison ,
.Ql passkey-entry ,
.Ql passkey-display
or
.Ql pin ;
.Ar passkey
is empty unless there is a number to show.
For
.Ql passkey-display
nothing needs answering; the pairing goes on in the daemon while the user
types the passkey on the device.
.It Cm confirm Ar device Cm yes | no | Ar passkey | Ar pin
Answer the prompt a pairing with
.Ar device
is waiting on:
.Cm yes
or
.Cm no
for a number to compare, the six digits the device shows for passkey
entry, or the PIN.
A prompt not answered within 60 seconds fails the pairing.
.It Cm connect Ar device
Connect a paired device and wait (up to 30 seconds) until it is connected.
A keyboard, mouse or other HID device, Classic or LE, gets its HID channels
and appears to the system as a HID device; anything else gets a link.
Prints
.Dq connected to Ar address
with the profiles that run over the link.
Paired devices normally reconnect by themselves; this is for when one has
not.
.It Cm disconnect Ar device
Disconnect a device: its profiles, then the link.
A keyboard or mouse that reconnects by itself comes back at its next key
press or movement; use
.Cm unpair
to stop that.
.It Cm unpair Ar device
Forget a paired device: its keys, its entry in the pairing store, and its
link if it is connected.
.It Cm device Ar device
Print one device's name, class, whether it is paired, its link state, the
controller it is connected through, its signal and the profiles running
over the link.
.It Cm autoconnect Ar device Cm on | off
Whether a paired device is reconnected when a controller powers up
(on by default).
.It Cm audio
Print the audio preferences and every device with audio now: a headset
played to (and the
.Pa /dev/dsp.btN
programs open), a phone playing to this machine, AVRCP, and the headset's
volume.
.Cm status
prints the same.
.It Cm audio Ar device Cm on | off
Whether
.Xr bluetoothd 8
plays to a paired headset when it connects (on by default).
Off stops a mixer that runs.
.It Cm audio Ar device Cm volume Ar level
Set a headset's volume, 0\(en127, with AVRCP absolute volume.
.It Cm audio Ar device Cm hfp Op Cm cvsd
Open a headset's microphone: switch it to the Hands-Free Profile, so its
.Pa /dev/dsp.btN
(and
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
when it owns it) records from the headset, for a call.
Speech is wide band (mSBC) when the headset and the controller allow it,
narrowband (CVSD) otherwise;
.Cm cvsd
holds it to narrowband.
Playback on HFP is speech quality.
Returns at once;
.Cm audio
shows when the microphone is on.
.It Cm audio Ar device Cm a2dp
Close the microphone and go back to stereo (A2DP).
.It Cm audio Cm autoswitch Cm on | off
Whether a headset that connects takes
.Pa /dev/dsp
(on by default).
Applies to the next headset that connects.
.It Cm audio Cm sink Cm on | off
Be a Bluetooth speaker, so a phone can play through this machine (off by
default).
.It Cm discoverable Cm on | off Oo Fl t Ar seconds Oc Op Ar node
Let other devices find this computer by inquiry.
With
.Fl t
it turns itself off again after
.Ar seconds
and the setting is not saved; without, it is saved as
.Ql DiscoverableState .
.It Cm info Op Ar node
Print what the controller says about itself: its address, manufacturer,
HCI and LMP versions, supported features, ACL, SCO and LE buffer sizes;
the HCI node's free credits and packet and byte counters; and every open
link with its type, handle, role, encryption, mode and pending packets.
.It Cm hci Oo Fl n Ar node Oc Ar ogf ocf Op Ar byte ...
Send one raw HCI command and print the event that answered it, its status
and its return parameters.
.Ar ogf ,
.Ar ocf
and each parameter
.Ar byte
are hexadecimal, with or without
.Ql 0x ;
multi-byte parameters are given in the controller's order, least
significant byte first.
For a command answered by Command Status, the command goes on in the
controller and its later events are not shown.
Only root may send raw commands, and
.Xr bluetoothd 8
is not told what they change.
.It Cm ping Oo Fl c Ar count Oc Oo Fl s Ar size Oc Oo Fl i Ar seconds Oc Ar device
Send L2CAP Echo Requests to a Classic device and print each round trip,
then a summary.
.Fl c
sends
.Ar count
(default 5; 0 until Ctrl-C),
.Fl s
puts
.Ar size
bytes in each (default 44, at most 668), and
.Fl i
waits
.Ar seconds
between them (default 1).
The device is paged if there is no link yet, so the first round trip is
slow, and it need not be paired.
LE has no echo, so LE devices cannot be pinged.
.It Cm sdp Ar device , Cm browse Ar device
Browse the SDP records of a Classic device, paired or not, and print every
attribute of every record.
.Xr bluetoothd 8
connects to the device, which may page it, reads the records and renders
them; well-known service classes, protocols and attributes are named.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/" -compact
.It Pa /usr/sbin/bluetooth
This command.
.It Pa /var/log/bluetoothd.stderr
The daemon's log.
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
(policy and paired devices, 0644) and
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
(link keys and LE keys in plaintext, 0600).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 2 -compact
.It 0
Success.
For
.Cm ping ,
at least one echo came back.
.It 1
An error, an HCI command that failed, or
.Xr bluetoothd 8
could not be reached.
.It 2
Usage error.
.It 3
.Cm pair
needs an answer and was told not to ask, or has no terminal to ask on; use
.Cm confirm .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Pair and connect a mouse, a keyboard and headphones:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# bluetooth scan
scanning for 10 seconds...
